\section{Data sources}{

\code{source = "arco"} reads ECMWF's Analysis-Ready Cloud-Optimised (ARCO) archive directly. It
requires an ECMWF API key and the optional \code{Rarr} and \code{ecmwfr} packages. \code{variable} uses the
same names as the hosted API, but the archive carries fewer of them: 20 for
\code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"}, 16 for \code{"land"}, and the 8 they share for \code{"both"}, plus the
derived \code{"altitude"}. Use \code{\link[=pressurepath_variable_available]{pressurepath_variable_available()}} to list them.

\code{source = "api"} uses the hosted GeoPressureAPI. It needs no ECMWF key or \code{Rarr} installation
and reaches the full Earth Engine band set: 292 variables for \code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"}
and \code{"both"}, 70 for \code{"land"}.

The default, \code{source = "auto"}, uses ARCO when a key stored by \code{\link[ecmwfr:wf_set_key]{ecmwfr::wf_set_key()}} is
available and GeoPressureAPI otherwise. Use \code{pressurepath_create_arco()} or
\code{pressurepath_create_api()} to select a backend explicitly.
}

\section{Path and ERA5 processing}{

Measurements are retained only when \code{path} contains the surrounding stationary periods.
Coordinates during flights are interpolated linearly, while ERA5 values are sampled at the
nearest grid cell and hour. \code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"} uses 0.25 degree global ERA5,
\code{"land"} uses 0.1 degree ERA5-Land, and \code{"both"} selects ERA5-Land over land and global ERA5
over water.

Surface pressure is returned in hPa and normalised to the mean tag pressure within each
stationary-period and elevation-label group. Observations labelled \code{"discard"} are excluded
from those means. If \code{solar_dep} is not \code{NULL}, local sunrise and sunset are added.
}

\section{Pressure-derived altitude}{

When altitude is produced, GeoPressureR uses the same barometric relation as
\href{https://github.com/GeoPressure/GeoPressureAPI}{GeoPressureAPI}:
\deqn{z_\mathrm{tag}=z_\mathrm{ERA5}+\frac{T_\mathrm{ERA5}}{L_b}\left[\left(
\frac{P_\mathrm{tag}}{P_\mathrm{ERA5}}\right)^{-R L_b/(g M)}-1\right].}
Here, \eqn{P_\mathrm{tag}} is tag pressure, while \eqn{P_\mathrm{ERA5}}, \eqn{T_\mathrm{ERA5}},
and \eqn{z_\mathrm{ERA5}}
are ERA5 surface pressure, 2 m temperature, and model-surface elevation. Model-surface elevation
is obtained from ERA5 surface geopotential divided by standard gravity. The constants are the
standard temperature lapse rate \eqn{L_b=-0.0065} K/m, universal gas constant
\eqn{R=8.31432} J/(mol K), standard gravity \eqn{g=9.80665} m/s², and molar mass of dry air
\eqn{M=0.0289644} kg/mol. Pressure is converted to Pa internally and altitude is returned in
metres above mean sea level.
}

\section{Choosing \code{era5_dataset}}{

\strong{Use \code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"} whenever altitude is computed.} It is the default.

ERA5-Land's \code{surface_pressure} is not the exact hydrostatic image of the orography ERA5-Land
publishes as \code{geopotential} — the two disagree by up to ~10 hPa in steep terrain. The
\eqn{z_\mathrm{ERA5}} term therefore fails to cancel from the relation above and the whole discrepancy
lands in the retrieved altitude. Measured against 41,653 hourly station-pressure observations
from 271 NOAA ISD stations (2–3576 m, Alps, July 2020), compared with surveyed station
elevation:\tabular{llll}{
   \code{era5_dataset} \tab bias \tab MAE \tab RMSE \cr
   \code{"single-levels"} \tab −0.6 m \tab 9.0 m \tab 27.4 m \cr
   \code{"land"} / \code{"both"} \tab +2.5 m \tab 55.3 m \tab 76.9 m \cr
}


ERA5-Land replays the land component of ERA5 and assimilates no atmospheric observations of its
own, so it carries no independent information about absolute altitude. Substituting the
orography implied by its own surface pressure removes the error exactly and reproduces
\code{"single-levels"} to 0.1 m.

\code{"land"} and \code{"both"} are retained for backward compatibility and are \strong{deprecated for
altitude} since GeoPressureR 3.7.0; requesting \code{"altitude"} with either signals a deprecation
warning. They remain fully supported and appropriate for retrieving other variables at 0.1
degree resolution, and \code{\link[=geopressure_map]{geopressure_map()}} still defaults to \code{"land"} because its pressure
mismatch is differential and unaffected.
}

\section{Accuracy}{

With \code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"} the altitude error separates into two parts that behave
very differently. A static per-site offset (median 3.7 m, p90 15 m) reflects station metadata
and sub-grid terrain and does not vary in time, while the temporal scatter (median SD 3.1 m,
p90 7.7 m) is the actual reanalysis error. Expect roughly \strong{3 m for relative altitude changes
at a fixed location} and \strong{10 m mean absolute error for absolute altitude}, degrading to tens
of metres in steep terrain — where it is a fixed offset rather than noise. Precision is nearly
independent of flight altitude: de-biased RMSE stays 2–7 m up to 1000 m above the model surface
and about 12 m at 1000–3000 m above it.
}

\section{A note on accumulated variables}{

\code{total_precipitation}, \code{surface_solar_radiation_downwards} and
\code{surface_thermal_radiation_downwards} do \strong{not} mean the same thing across backends when
\code{era5_dataset = "land"}. GeoPressureAPI serves Earth Engine's ERA5-Land collection, where these
bands are overwritten by their \verb{*_hourly} counterparts and are therefore hourly increments.
ARCO serves ECMWF's own ERA5-Land fields, which accumulate from 00 UTC. For
\code{era5_dataset = "single-levels"} both are hourly and agree. Take the difference between
consecutive hours if you need increments from ARCO over land.
}

\section{ECMWF API key}{

ARCO access and CDS downloads use the same ECMWF Personal Access Token. Create an ECMWF account,
copy the token from your \href{https://cds.climate.copernicus.eu/profile}{Climate Data Store profile},
accept the dataset licence terms, and store the token once in your system keyring:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ecmwfr::wf_set_key("your-personal-access-token")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Do not put the token in a script or commit it to version control. GeoPressureR retrieves the
stored token with \code{\link[ecmwfr:wf_get_key]{ecmwfr::wf_get_key()}} for all ECMWF-backed functions, including
\code{\link[=geopressure_timeseries]{geopressure_timeseries()}}, \code{\link[=pressurepath_create]{pressurepath_create()}}, and \code{\link[=tag_download_wind]{tag_download_wind()}}.
}

